  "worked_solution": "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet was vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(V\\cap D),$ we take the number of dogs who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(12)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(34+29=63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap D)=\\frac{12}{63}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(V\\cap {D}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of cats who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(15)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap {D}^{\u2032})=\\frac{15}{63}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{63}+\\frac{15}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
